tatistical power and reliability. For instance, meta-analyses have confirmed the efficacy of acupuncture combined with medication and the effectiveness of psychological therapies like biofeedback and cognitive behavioral therapy. The integration of these methods into clinical evaluation processes ensures that recommendations are based on high-quality, aggregated evidence. [Data: Entities (237, 246); Relationships (384, 372, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Systematic reviews and meta-analyses provide high-level evidence for non-drug migraine treatments\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The concept of biobehavioral therapy encompasses a range of non-drug interventions—including cognitive behavioral therapy, biofeedback, and relaxation therapy—that target both psychological and physiological factors in migraine. These therapies are recommended as part of a holistic, patient-centered approach to migraine management. Their inclusion under a unified framework highlights the importance of addressing the mind-body connection in chronic pain conditions. The evidence supports their use as both standalone and combined treatments, particularly for patients with poor medication tolerance or adherence. [Data: Entities (610, 607); Relationships (897, 900, 401, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Biobehavioral therapy integrates psychological and physiological approaches to migraine management\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Dietary and lifestyle modifications, such as dietary regulation and exercise, are recognized as essential components of non-drug migraine management. These interventions are particularly valuable for patients with poor medication adherence or those who experience adverse effects from pharmacological treatments. While specific dietary triggers vary by individual, general recommendations include avoiding known triggers and maintaining regular eating patterns. These strategies are part of a broader non-pharmacological toolkit that supports long-term migraine control and improved patient autonomy. [Data: Entities (234, 233); Relationships (365, 364, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Dietary and lifestyle changes are key non-drug strategies in migraine prevention\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The integration of multiple non-drug therapies—such as combining acupuncture with medication, or using CBT with biofeedback—demonstrates a shift toward personalized, multi-modal treatment plans. This approach acknowledges the complex, multifactorial nature of migraine and allows for tailored interventions based on patient needs, comorbidities, and treatment preferences. Evidence from meta-analyses and randomized trials supports the synergistic effects of combining therapies, leading to greater reductions in migraine frequency and improved quality of life compared to single-modality treatments. [Data: Entities (237, 254, 255, 232, 235, 253); Relationships (372, 401, 405, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Combining non-drug therapies enhances treatment efficacy through multi-modal approaches\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 8.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity is high due to the strong evidence base and clinical relevance of non-pharmacological migraine treatments, which are increasingly central to patient care and public health guidelines.\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community is centered on non-pharmacological interventions for migraine management, including biofeedback, acupuncture, cognitive behavioral therapy, mindfulness, aerobic exercise, and yoga. These entities are interconnected through a robust network of evidence from systematic reviews, meta-analyses, and randomized trials, supporting their use as effective, safe, and complementary alternatives or adjuncts to medication. The integration of psychological, physiological, and lifestyle-based therapies forms a comprehensive, biobehavioral framework that addresses th